% ============================================================================
% sign-shape — the shape the federal standard specifies for a traffic sign
% (adj-facts-stdlib, TRANSPORTATION).
% ============================================================================
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? sign_shape(stop_sign, $S)
%       % octagon
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `sign_shape(sign, shape)` carrying the source citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query. The BACKWARD query is
% the one a child actually asks — "which sign is the eight-sided one?" —
% and `? sign_shape($Sign, octagon)` answers it.
%
% THIRD transportation library, joining `traffic-lights.adj` and
% `green-signal-permitted-movement.adj`, which cite the same publisher: the
% FHWA's Manual on Uniform Traffic Control Devices (MUTCD), the federal
% standard for what American road signs must look like.
%
% *** THIS TABLE CONTAINS A DEFAULT AND ITS OWN EXCEPTIONS, AND THAT IS
% WHY THE HEDGES ARE LOAD-BEARING RATHER THAN DECORATIVE. ***
%
% The MUTCD states two shape DEFAULTS, each explicitly defeasible:
%
%     "Regulatory signs shall be rectangular unless specifically
%      designated otherwise."
%     "Except as provided in Paragraph 2 or unless specifically designated
%      otherwise, all warning signs shall be diamond-shaped ..."
%
% and then designates otherwise, repeatedly. STOP and YIELD are BOTH
% regulatory signs, and neither is rectangular — one is an octagon, the
% other a downward-pointing equilateral triangle. NO PASSING ZONE is a
% warning sign that is not a diamond.
%
% So the rows would CONTRADICT EACH OTHER if the defaults were recorded
% flatly. They do not contradict, because each default carries its
% defeasibility inside its own atom —
% `rectangular_unless_specifically_designated_otherwise` — following the
% placement rule `veto-override.adj` and `karst-process-zone.adj` apply. A
% query for a bare unqualified `rectangular` therefore ABSTAINS, which is
% correct: the MUTCD never says regulatory signs ARE rectangular, and its
% own next sections are the proof.
%
% This is the clearest case yet for that rule. In earlier libraries the
% hedge preserved a shade of confidence; here, dropping it would make the
% library state a falsehood that the same document refutes two sections
% later.
%
% Provenance: five sentences from two CHAPTERS of the 2009 MUTCD — Chapter
% 2B, "Regulatory Signs, Barricades, and Gates", and Chapter 2C, "Warning
% Signs And Object Markers", both within Part 2, "Signs". The `source`
% envelope carries the regulatory default from Chapter 2B; the
% corroborations carry STOP (Section 2B.05) and YIELD (Section 2B.08) from
% the same chapter, and the warning default (2C.03) and the pennant
% exception (2C.45) from Chapter 2C, which has its OWN locator.
%
% Those section numbers matter to the argument rather than being decoration:
% STOP and YIELD sit inside the REGULATORY chapter, which is what makes them
% counterexamples to the regulatory default rather than unrelated signs, and
% NO PASSING ZONE sits inside the WARNING chapter for the same reason.
%
% THE WARNING-SIGN CITATION BEGINS WITH ITS EXCEPTION CLAUSE, DELIBERATELY.
% The sentence in the manual opens "Except as provided in Paragraph 2 or
% unless specifically designated otherwise, ..." and only then reaches
% "all warning signs shall be diamond-shaped". Quoting from "all warning
% signs" would have been a shorter, tidier citation that DROPPED the hedge
% from the evidence itself — leaving an atom that says "unless designated
% otherwise" backed by a quotation that appears to say no such thing. The
% citation has to carry the qualifier it is being used to justify.
%
% `trust authoritative` — the FHWA is the first-party federal publisher of
% the MUTCD, and this is the standard itself rather than a description of
% it. Both pages were CONTENT-verified by RAW TEXT EXTRACTION (HTTP 200,
% 589 and 406 substantive sentences, zero soft-404 markers), and every
% quoted sentence was confirmed to appear verbatim in that extraction.
%
% Provenance is TABLE-level, so all five rows carry all five sentences and
% `--explain` shows only the primary one — meaning the STOP row displays
% under the regulatory-default sentence, which does not ground it. That is
% a property of every multi-sentence table in this stdlib, tracked as issue
% #13898; the JSON output is the authoritative view.
%
% Honest abstention:
%
%   - BARE `rectangular` and bare `diamond_shaped` are NOT values. Asking
%     which sign is simply rectangular is asking for a claim the manual
%     declines to make, and this table declines with it.
%   - SPEED LIMIT, ONE WAY, DO NOT ENTER and the rest of the regulatory
%     family get no rows. They are governed by the rectangular default,
%     but the default is defeasible and this library records only shapes
%     the manual states for a NAMED sign. Inferring "rectangular" for a
%     specific sign from the general rule is exactly the reasoning-as-
%     recall this stdlib exists to prevent, and the STOP and YIELD rows
%     are standing proof that the inference is unsound.
%   - The RAILROAD CROSSBUCK, school-zone pentagon, and the interstate
%     shield are real shaped signs specified elsewhere in the manual. They
%     are not in the five sentences quoted here, so they are not rows; a
%     later slice can add them from their own sections.
%   - COLOR is not a value of this relation, though the quoted sentences
%     state it. Sign colour is a different axis and deserves its own
%     table rather than being smuggled into a shape column.
%
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table sign_shape {
    columns sign, shape

    row (regulatory_sign, rectangular_unless_specifically_designated_otherwise)
    row (stop_sign, octagon)
    row (yield_sign, downward_pointing_equilateral_triangle)

    row (warning_sign, diamond_shaped_unless_specifically_designated_otherwise)
    row (no_passing_zone_sign, pennant_shaped_isosceles_triangle)

    source "Regulatory signs shall be rectangular unless specifically designated otherwise."
    locator "https://mutcd.fhwa.dot.gov/htm/2009/part2/part2b.htm"
    trust authoritative
    cites "The STOP sign shall be an octagon with a white legend and border on a red background." locator "https://mutcd.fhwa.dot.gov/htm/2009/part2/part2b.htm"
    cites "The YIELD (R1-2) sign (see Figure 2B-1) shall be a downward-pointing equilateral triangle with a wide red border and the legend YIELD in red on a white background." locator "https://mutcd.fhwa.dot.gov/htm/2009/part2/part2b.htm"
    cites "Except as provided in Paragraph 2 or unless specifically designated otherwise, all warning signs shall be diamond-shaped (square with one diagonal vertical) with a black legend and border on a yellow background." locator "https://mutcd.fhwa.dot.gov/htm/2009/part2/part2c.htm"
    cites "The NO PASSING ZONE (W14-3) sign (see Figure 2C-8) shall be a pennant-shaped isosceles triangle with its longer axis horizontal and pointing to the right." locator "https://mutcd.fhwa.dot.gov/htm/2009/part2/part2c.htm"
}
